# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEXT IS LF IN THE INDEX AND MUST BE LF IN THE WORKING TREE TOO.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine and nothing here declared otherwise, so text files came out
# of a clone — or out of `git worktree add` — as CRLF, while a file an editor had just written was
# still LF. The same file therefore had two different byte sequences depending on how it got there.
#
# That is not cosmetic in this repository, because a lot of its tests READ SOURCE. They have to: the
# properties worth defending (the narrowed retry names no additive column, the bootstrap and the
# hand-run .sql say the same thing, no job kind is enqueued without a worker) cannot be reached
# without a database, so they are checked by reading the statement. Every one of those scans anchors
# on `\n`.
#
# On 2026-08-24 that cost fifteen failing tests in two files. They passed for whoever wrote them and
# failed on every clean checkout, with an error — "could not find the DDL template literal in
# schema.ts" — that points at the module rather than at the line endings. Roughly a third of the
# working tree was CRLF and two thirds LF at the time.
#
# Setting this changes NO content: the index is already LF throughout, so git normalises on the way
# in and `git status` stays clean. It only stops the conversion on the way out. Verified before
# committing — with this line present, git reports zero files modified.
#
# IT MUST STAY ABOVE THE BINARY BLOCK. Later rules win in .gitattributes, so `* text=auto` placed
# after `*.png binary` would re-enable text conversion for every one of those types — including the
# face weights the block below exists to protect.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets that must never have their line endings rewritten.
#
# The face-recognition weights are 6.4 MB of float32. Git detected them as binary on this checkout
# and left them alone — verified byte-for-byte against the working tree after committing — but that
# detection is heuristic and a future file could be misread. A weights file with CRLF substituted
# through it does not fail loudly: it loads, produces different descriptors, and every enrolled face
# quietly stops matching. That failure looks exactly like the employees being wrong, which is the
# most expensive kind of bug this product could have in its identity path.
#
# So it is declared rather than inferred.
*.bin binary
*.onnx binary
*.pt binary
*.safetensors binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
